{
    "title": "Regulatory Relief for Certain Stationary Sources To Promote American Coke Oven Processing Security",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "November 26, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-26/pdf/2025-21418.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has granted certain coke oven plants an extra two years to meet newly tightened pollution rules, saying the required technology isn\u2019t yet available at a workable cost and that uninterrupted coke and steel production is important for national security. During this extension, the listed facilities will keep following the previous standards that applied before the 2024 rule.\n\nWho stands to benefit? Coke producers and the steelmakers who rely on them get breathing room to avoid rushed shutdowns, costly retrofits, or supply disruptions. That could help protect jobs in steel communities, stabilize production for construction and defense projects, and give companies more time to engineer, test, and finance upgrades. Local economies around the affected plants may also see steadier operations while firms plan their compliance path.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Nearby communities and workers concerned about air quality could face a longer wait for the newer controls the 2024 rule called for, including tighter leak limits and added monitoring. Public health groups may argue the delay prolongs exposure to hazardous emissions and weakens momentum for cleaner industrial practices. Companies that make pollution control and monitoring equipment could see slower demand. The move may also spark legal and policy debates over how far the government should go in easing timelines when technology is considered not yet ready.\n\nIn short, the proclamation favors short\u2011term supply and security needs for coke and steel by pausing some environmental requirements. Supporters see it as practical and necessary; critics see added environmental and health risks. The next two years will likely determine whether industry, regulators, and communities can find a workable path that protects both production and public health.",
